  n = 13 experimental points from 2 papers, vf 0.30-0.60.
  MAPE against the K46 curve            : 341%  (dominated by grade mismatch -- these
     records are lighter S22/S32/S38 balloons and multi-layer graded foams, not epoxy/K46.)
  MAPE with grade-matched particle HP-MT : 202%  (HP-DS 203%), n=11
  Experimental points inside their grade-matched HS band: 0/11
  Mean signed error (grade-matched HP-MT - experiment) = +202%
  Interpretation: even grade-matched, the analytical estimates lie ABOVE every measurement, and several
  measured moduli fall BELOW the HS lower bound -- which no microstructure of perfectly bonded intact
  balloons in dense epoxy can do. The usual causes are matrix porosity, weak/debonded interfaces and
  balloon breakage during mixing/cure, plus the fact that reported 'compressive modulus' is often a
  machine-compliance-affected secant slope. Literature typically reports experiment 20-40% below HP-MT;
  the gap here is larger because these records are 0.6 vf graded laminates. No constant was tuned.
  Written: experimental_comparison.csv
  Written: modulus_vs_vf.png

-- Summary ------------------------------------------------------------------
  HP-MT (primary model): E rises from 3000 MPa at vf=0 to 4536 MPa at vf=0.60,
  while density falls from 1.180 to 0.748 g/cm^3 (specific modulus 6064 -> vs 2542 MPa/(g/cm^3) for neat epoxy).
  Uncertainty: model spread HP-MT vs HP-DS <= 0.6%; the HS band is much wider
  and is a bound, not an uncertainty; experiment scatters below both estimates (see MAPE above).
